So!  After playing casually, off and on for a few years, generally
dying before reaching the end of the gnomish mines or completing
sokoban, I decided to give the game another shot with a fresh
approach, and I'm sure glad I did.

In the past, I had experimented with most classes, but generally
settled on playing samurai or monks.  I had a poor grasp of strategy
and no concept of identification beyond reading ID scrolls or trying
things out.  I would delve too deep too quickly and get in over my
head.

When I began playing again a month ago, I did some research.  Armed
with the spoilers I gleaned from the various available websites (and
this newsgroup), I realized that the best way to learn survival is
probably to play a class that requires caution from the start.  For a
couple of weeks I played protection racket healers, then moved up to
wizards...  then decided to attempt a class I never recall playing:
the Valkyrie.

The Valkyrie's reputation as the easiest class in the game is well-
deserved.  My first Valkyrie completed the mines before dying at XL12
at the hands of a mumak.  My second Valkyrie was very fortunate: with
the early discovery of a ring of slow digestion, I succeeded at the
protection racket, and looted +2 rustproof gauntlets of power from a
shop.  After clearing out most of the mines and scoring some good
armor, I had reached XL9, but found no fountains except in Minetown.
I had heard that dipping for Excalibur in Minetown was a bad idea, but
I figured that with an armor class of -18 and a level 9 pet I could
handle a dainty elven guard.  DYWYPI?

I'm on my third Valkyrie now, who is now XL 16, and I'm much deeper in
the dungeon than ever before.  I've taken my time to identify as much
as possible, and I don't engage a foe in melee unless I'm positive I
can handle it.  I finally understand that this game is not impossible;
it's just very hard ^_^.  I'm going to ask RGRN for advice on my
current game, but that's for another post.

Happy Hacking,

